          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            As described at
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation+between+type+theory+and+category+theory"} relation between type theory and category theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            there is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalence
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Theories
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ⇄
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi Categories
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Theories \;\rightleftarrows\; Categories
          %p
            where on the left we have a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theories"} theories
            of some sort (i.e. in some
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/doctrine"} doctrine
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"} cartesian closure
          %p
            The
            %strong syntactic category
            construction is the functor from theories to categories, denoted
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Syn
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Syn
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Con
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Con
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/walking+structure"} walking
            model of that theory, i.e. a structured category of the appropriate sort which is generated by a model of that theory. Since the objects of the syntactic category are frequently taken to be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contexts"} contexts
            in the theory, the syntactic category is also called the
            = succeed "." do
              %strong category of contexts
          %p
            The functor in the other direction associates to any category its
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %strong syntactic category
            or
            %strong category of contexts
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Con
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Con(T)
            is defined as follows.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Its
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
                are the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contexts"} contexts
                in the type theory;
            %li
              %p
                Its
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
                between contexts are
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/substitutions"} substitutions
                %strong interpretations
                of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variables"} variables
          %p
            A morphism from the context
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            to the context
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            consists of a way of fulfilling the assumptions required by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            by appropriately interpreting those provided by
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            for variables needed in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            and proving whatever is necessary from the assumptions at hand.
          %p
            More precisely, let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            be contexts of some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 0
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi …
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi n
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \;=\; x_0:A_0,\; x_1:A_1(x_0),\; x_2:A_2(x_0,x_1),\;\dots x_n:A_n(x_0,\dots,x_{n-1})
          %p and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mn 0
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mn 1
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mn 2
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi …
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mi m
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mrow
                      %mi m
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Delta \;=\; y_0:B_0,\; y_1:B_1(y_0),\; y_2:B_2(y_0,y_1),\;\dots y_m:B_m(y_0,\dots,y_{m-1})
                  \\,.
          %p
            (Here we allow the possibility that each type in these contexts
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type"} depends
            on the variables occurring earlier in the context, but for simplicity we can ignore that. )
          %p
            Then a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo →
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \to \Delta
            in the category of contexts
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Con
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Con(T)
            consists of a sequence of terms such as the following:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Γ
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊢
                          %msub
                            %mi t
                            %mn 0
                          %mo :
                          %msub
                            %mi B
                            %mn 0
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Γ
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊢
                          %msub
                            %mi t
                            %mn 1
                          %mo :
                          %msub
                            %mi B
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi t
                            %mn 0
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi ⋮
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Γ
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊢
                          %msub
                            %mi t
                            %mi m
                          %mo :
                          %msub
                            %mi B
                            %mi m
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi t
                            %mn 0
                          %mo ,
                          %msub
                            %mi t
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %mi …
                          %mo ,
                          %msub
                            %mi t
                            %mrow
                              %mi m
                              %mo −
                              %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{aligned}
                  \\Gamma &amp;\vdash t_0 :B_0\\
                  \\Gamma &amp;\vdash t_1 : B_1(t_0)\\
                  \\vdots\\
                  \\Gamma &amp;\vdash t_m : B_m(t_0,t_1,\dots, t_{m-1})
                  \\end{aligned}
          %p
            In other words, to give such a morphism we must give, for each type (or assumption) required by
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
          .query
            %p This might fit better after the motivating examples below; but maybe those examples don't make sense to a newcomer. This is incomplete, however, since it doesn't address contexts that include propositional hypotheses. —Toby
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#structure Structure
          %p
            Depending on the type-forming operations available in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Con
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Con(T)
            will have categorical structure. Roughly:
          %ul
            %li
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              has
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+types"} function types
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Con
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Con(T)
              is
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"} cartesian closed category
            %li
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              has
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sum+types"} sum types
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Con
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Con(T)
              has binary
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproducts"} coproducts
            %li
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
              with
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+product+types"} dependent product types
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Con
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Con(T)
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+category"} locally cartesian closed category
            %li
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              is a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+theory"} regular theory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Con
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Con(T)
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+category"} regular category
            %li
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              is a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+theory"} coherent theory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Con
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Con(T)
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+category"} coherent category
            %li
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              is a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+theory"} geometric theory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Con
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Con(T)
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+category"} geometric category
            %li
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              is a finitary
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+theory"} first-order theory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Con
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Con(T)
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+category"} Heyting category
          %p
            And so on. (If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            lacks
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eta-conversion"} eta-conversion
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Con
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Con(T)
              always has finite
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/products"} products
          %p
            This is due to the objects of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Con
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Con(T)
            being contexts rather than types. A way to avoid this is to work instead with a syntactic
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+multicategory"} cartesian multicategory
          %p
            In fact,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Con
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Con(T)
            is not just a structured category, it is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/split+model+of+type+theory"} split model of type theory
            in any of the senses described there. In constructing formally an internal logic that involves dependent types, this is important to keep track of.
          %h3#UniversalProperty Universal property
          %p
            The syntactic category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Con
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Con(T)
            has the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+property"} universal property
            that for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            any suitable category,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Con
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Con(T) \to C
          %p
            that preserve the relevant structure correspond to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} interpretations
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p The construction
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Con
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi TypeTheories
                  %mo →
                  %mi Categories
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Con\colon TypeTheories \to Categories
          %p
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
            in an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
            that
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation+between+type+theory+and+category+theory"} relates type theories and categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Lan
                  %mo :
                  %mi Categories
                  %mo →
                  %mi TypeTheories
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Lan : Categories \to TypeTheories
            extracts the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            of a category.
          %p
            Accordingly, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/counit+of+an+adjunction"} adjunction counit
            evaluated on any category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Con
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Lan
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Con(Lan(C)) \to C
          %p
            says that there a canonical interpretation of the internal logic of a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            itself, while the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+of+an+adjunction"} unit
            evaluated at a theory
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo →
                  %mi Lan
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Con
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \to Lan(Con(T))
          %p
            says that there is a canonical interpretation of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            in the internal logic of its syntactic category.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#SubstitutionAndIntroductionOfTerm Substitution and introduction of a single term
          %p
            Given a context
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            and a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\Gamma, x : X]
          %p
            Given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term"} term
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t : X
            there is a canonical morphism of contexts
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Gamma \stackrel{[t/x]}{\to} [\Gamma, x : X]
          %p
            which picks that term in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+change"} base change
            functor along this morphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo *
                  %mo :
                  %mi Con
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mi Con
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [t/x]^* : Con(T)_{/[\Gamma, x : X]} \to Con(T)_{/\Gamma}
          %p
            is the operation of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/substitution"} substitution
              of
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variables"} variables
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type"} dependent type
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma, x : X \vdash P(x)
              to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash P(t)
          %p There is also the canonical morphism of contexts
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\hat x : [\Gamma, x : X] \to \Gamma
          %p
            which simply forgets the type
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+change"} base change
            along this morphism is the context extension functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mover
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mo *
                  %mo :
                  %mi Con
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi Γ
                  %mo →
                  %mi Con
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\hat x^* : Con(T)_{/\Gamma} \to Con(T)_{/[\Gamma , x : X]}
                  \\,.
          %p
            Its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+product"} dependent product
            functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prod_{x : X}
            giving the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+quantifier"} universal quantifier
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∀
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo :
                        %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall_{x : X}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+sum"} dependent sum
            functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_{x : X}
            giving the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/existential+quantifier"} existential quantifier
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∃
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo :
                        %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists_{x :X}
          %h3#group The theory of a group
          %p
            For example, consider these contexts in the theory of a group
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi G
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \;=\; a\colon G,\; b\colon G
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi G
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi G
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mi b
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi a
                    %mn 2
                  %msup
                    %mi b
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta \;=\; a\colon G,\; b\colon G,\; (a b)^2 = a^2 b^2
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi G
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi G
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi a
                  %mi b
                  %mo =
                  %mi b
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \;=\; a\colon G,\; b\colon G,\; a b = b a
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi G
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z \;=\; x\colon G,\; y\colon G
          %p
            One interpretation of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            (that is, a morphism from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            to
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi b
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi b
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \coloneqq a,\; b \coloneqq b .
          %p
            The fact that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mi b
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi a
                    %mn 2
                  %msup
                    %mi b
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a b)^2 = a^2 b^2
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            is ignored. In type-theoretic language, this would consist of the two terms
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi a
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                          %mi G
                          %mo ,
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi b
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                          %mi G
                          %mo ,
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi a
                          %mi b
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mn 2
                          %mo =
                          %msup
                            %mi a
                            %mn 2
                          %msup
                            %mi b
                            %mn 2
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi a
                          %mo :
                          %mi G
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi a
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                          %mi G
                          %mo ,
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi b
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                          %mi G
                          %mo ,
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi a
                          %mi b
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mn 2
                          %mo =
                          %msup
                            %mi a
                            %mn 2
                          %msup
                            %mi b
                            %mn 2
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi b
                          %mo :
                          %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{aligned}
                  a\colon G,\; b\colon G,\; (a b)^2 = a^2 b^2 &amp;\vdash a:G\\
                  a\colon G,\; b\colon G,\; (a b)^2 = a^2 b^2 &amp;\vdash b:G
                  \\end{aligned}
          %p
            Note that in this way of presenting things, the names of the variables in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            do not appear; only the order in which the types appear in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            matters.
          %p
            A less obvious interpretation of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            is the substitution
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi b
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi a
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \coloneqq b,\; b \coloneqq a .
          %p
            There is no reason to keep variable names the same. (At this point, compare
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            and
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
          %p
            Another, perhaps even less obvious, morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo →
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta \to \Gamma
            is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ≔
                  %msup
                    %mi a
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi b
                  %mo ≔
                  %msup
                    %mi a
                    %mn 3
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \coloneqq a^2,\; b \coloneqq a^3 .
          %p
            Not only does this ignore that
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mi b
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi a
                      %mn 2
                    %msup
                      %mi b
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a b)^2 = a^2 b^2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            more than once. Ignoring and reusing information like this is not always allowed in substructural logics such as
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
          %p
            We can interpret
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            without renaming variables because the theory of a group allows us to derive the judgment
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi G
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi G
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mi b
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi a
                    %mn 2
                  %msup
                    %mi b
                    %mn 2
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi a
                  %mi b
                  %mo =
                  %mi b
                  %mi a
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\colon G,\; b\colon G,\; (a b)^2 = a^2 b^2 \;\vdash\; a b = b a.
          %p
            That is, we get a morphism from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            by performing the substitution
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi b
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \coloneqq a,\; b \coloneqq b
          %p
            and then inserting a proof of the above judgment. As it happens, the argument in such a proof is reversible, so you should expect that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            are also isomorphic.
          %p
            Are there any morphisms from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            to
            = succeed "?" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi b
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \coloneqq a,\; b \coloneqq a
          %p and then inserting a proof that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mi a
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi a
                    %mn 2
                  %msup
                    %mi a
                    %mn 2
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \;\vdash\; (a a)^2 = a^2 a^2 .
          %p
            All the same, we would not want to say that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            are isomorphic contexts; although there are morphisms in each direction, composing them should never produce identity morphisms on both sides.
          %p
            The category structure of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Con
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Con(T)
            can be seen explicitly as well. First, given a context
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+morphism"} identity
            morphism where every variable is substituted for itself and every statement assumed is proved immediately from itself.
          %p
            Given morphisms
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi f
                    %mi Δ
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi g
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \overset{f}\to \Delta \overset{g}\to E
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composite"} composite
            as follows: First, for each variable
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            required by
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            tells us how to substitute a term
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            built out of the variables in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            tells us how to substitute a term from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            for each of these variables. So in the end,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is expressed as a term
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T[g]
            involving variables available in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
          %p
            To really complete the definition of a category, I should also describe when two morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo →
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \to \Delta
            are
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equal
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            can be proved equal in the context
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
          %p
            Now you should be able to prove that composition satisfies the axioms of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
          %p Notice that the exact definition of equality of morphisms can depend heavily on the theory in question and your own purposes. For example, this definition makes sense only because we have a notion of proving equality of elements of a group. Also, you can sometimes place interesting conditions on whether two proofs count as equivalent, rather than requiring either syntactic identity or (as we do here) accepting proof irrelevance.
          .num_example
            %h6#exercise Exercise
            %p
              Now that the category of contexts (in one sense) of the theory of a group has been completely defined, describe that category (up to
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grp"} category of groups
          .proof
            %h6#answer Answer
            %p
              In
              %a{:href => "http://www.rot13.com/"} rot13
              (so that you have a chance to think about it yourself without accidentally seeing the answer): gur bccbfvgr bs gur pngrtbel bs svavgryl cerfragrq tebhcf.
          %p
            The result of this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exercise"} exercise
            is true in more generality: it works for any finite-limit theory; see in particular
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theory"} Lawvere theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Elephant"} Elephant
          %h2#variations Variations
          %h3#cartesian_multicategories Cartesian multicategories
          %p
            Instead of a syntactic category, for a non-dependent type theory one can construct instead a syntactic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+multicategory"} cartesian multicategory
            (or, in the case of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear
            type theory, a plain (symmetric)
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multicategory"} multicategory
          %h3#the_syntactic_site The syntactic site
          %p
            For some doctrines, the syntactic category of any theory is naturally equipped with the structure of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            is a regular, coherent, or geometric theory, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Con
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Con(T)
            is a regular, coherent, or geometric category, which comes with a naturally defined topology. When equipped with this topology, the syntactic category is called the
            = succeed "." do
              %strong syntactic site
          %p
            In each of these cases, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sheaves"} category of sheaves
            on the syntactic site is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+topos"} classifying topos
            of the theory. In other words, it has the universal property that for any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} Grothendieck topos
            = succeed "," do
